title: How shared buffer hits fragment read combining in Postgres
source: hn
url: https://frn.sh/postgres-io/
date: '2026-11-23'
authors:
- Fernando Simões
why_read: Read this to understand the historical trade-offs in Postgres sequential scanning and why cached pages can fragment disk reads. You will gain a clear mental model of how buffer rings and readahead evolved over major releases.
hn_id: '50611113'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50611113
image: /infographics/09-hn-50611113.jpg
---

Postgres sequential scans have undergone a fascinating evolution from basic syscalls to coordinated asynchronous streaming reads. When a table dwarfs the shared buffer pool by five to ten times, naive sequential scans trash the cache, evicting hot pages without providing real utility.

The initial fix, merged back in Postgres 8.3, used small buffer rings to bound scan impact. However, an unexpected consequence emerged: issuing single 8 KiB page reads could split contiguous disk reads in half whenever an intermediate page already resided in memory, adding avoidable syscall overhead.

Over the years, solutions progressed from posix_fadvise prefetching in bitmap heap scans to contemporary work on unified read streams and asynchronous I/O. Modern engines require scatter-gather reads and tight kernel coordination to saturated high-throughput NVMe drives without locking up buffer locks.

Understanding how your database engine schedules disk I/O will transform how you tune concurrency parameters and buffer sizing under heavy scan workloads.

