Standard Bloom filters are no longer the most space-efficient choice for immutable database files. When data is written once and read millions of times, such as in an LSM-tree SSTable or a bad-domain blocklist, binary fuse filters offer a significant leap forward in storage density.

A binary fuse filter provides the exact same false-positive guarantees as a classic Bloom filter but reduces memory consumption by roughly twenty-five percent. For a dataset of one million items at a 0.39 percent false-positive rate, a fuse filter requires only nine bits per key compared to twelve bits for a tuned Bloom filter.

The core architectural trade-off is mutability. Binary fuse filters require the entire key set upfront during construction, which makes subsequent inserts impossible without rebuilding the structure from scratch.

For storage engines that already write read-only data blocks to disk, this immutability constraint is not a liability. It represents free memory savings and tighter cache residency across distributed nodes.

Defaulting to classic Bloom filters in read-heavy immutable pipelines leaves valuable memory on the table.

