When using Redis Streams consumer groups for distributed workers, messages that are delivered but unacknowledged live in the Pending Entries List (PEL). If your worker crashes during execution and the stream gets trimmed past that message ID, what happens to the pending state?

The PEL keeps tracking the message ID even after the actual stream item has been trimmed from memory. If another worker attempts to claim that message using XCLAIM or inspect it via XPENDING, it receives a valid reference to an entry that no longer exists in the stream payload.

Failing to handle this specific edge case will cause silent progress loss and broken consumer recovery loops. Always verify stream retention buffers against worst-case worker downtime when designing asynchronous pipeline checkpoints.

