Fully homomorphic encryption remains notoriously slow for production systems, but somewhat homomorphic encryption hits a practical architectural sweet spot. By restricting operations to degree-2 polynomials, systems can compute vector dot products, cosine similarities, and euclidean distances directly over encrypted data.

Under the hood, the trade-offs between implementations are stark. Schemes like Boneh-Goh-Nissim and Freeman operate on elliptic curves with bilinear pairings, whereas lattice-based alternatives like BV and GHV rely on polynomial and matrix multiplication. These differing foundations cause ciphertext sizes, noise growth, and computational overhead to diverge by orders of magnitude.

Understanding these algorithmic boundaries allows systems engineers to execute privacy-preserving vector search without incurring the crippling latency of full bootstrapping.

